[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem: Show that the set of real numbers x satisfying[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\sum_{k=1}^{70} \frac{k}{x-k} \ge \frac{5}{4}$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis a union of disjoint intervals, the sum of whose lengths is 1988.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me first check for answer leaks - the problem statement is clean, just the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproblem. No leaked solutions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me define $f(x) = \sum_{k=1}^{70} \frac{k}{x-k}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe function has vertical asymptotes at $x = 1, 2, \ldots, 70$. On each interval[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$(k, k+1)$ for $k = 1, \ldots, 69$, and on $(-\infty, 1)$ and $(70, \infty)$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe function is continuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the behavior. For $x > 70$, all terms are positive, and $f$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis decreasing (each term $k/(x-k)$ is decreasing). As $x \to 70^+$, $f \to +[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\infty$. As $x \to \infty$, $f \to 0$. So on $(70, \infty)$, $f$ decreases from [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$+\infty$ to $0$, crossing $5/4$ exactly once.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $x < 1$, all terms are negative, so $f(x) < 0 < 5/4$. No solutions there.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOn each interval $(k, k+1)$ for $k = 1, \ldots, 69$: As $x \to k^+$, the term [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$k/(x-k) \to +\infty$, so $f \to +\infty$. As $x \to (k+1)^-$, the term $(k+1)/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(x-(k+1)) \to -\infty$, so $f \to -\infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIs $f$ monotonically decreasing on each interval? $f'(x) = -\sum_{k=1}^{70} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\frac{k}{(x-k)^2} < 0$. Yes! $f$ is strictly decreasing on each interval where [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mit's continuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo on each interval $(k, k+1)$, $f$ decreases from $+\infty$ to $-\infty$, [0m
